WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.034 --> 00:00:11.795
The noise of the crowd was tremendous as the four young lads from Liverpool, a port city in

2
00:00:11.855 --> 00:00:14.296
northwestern England, stepped out of their plane.

3
00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:21.258
John Lennon, Paul McCartney, George Harrison, and Ringo Starr were all members of the radical new pop band

4
00:00:21.538 --> 00:00:24.899
the Beatles, and this was their first trip to the United States.

5
00:00:25.719 --> 00:00:30.572
More than three thousand girls had lined up at Kennedy International Airport in New York City to greet

6
00:00:30.592 --> 00:00:31.593
the handsome quartet.

7
00:00:32.194 --> 00:00:34.956
They were screaming, shouting, and crying with excitement.

8
00:00:35.677 --> 00:00:40.481
Many held up signs with messages to the young men, such as "I love you, please stay!"

9
00:00:40.961 --> 00:00:42.563
and "Welcome to America."

10
00:00:42.963 --> 00:00:44.184
The frenzy was incredible.

11
00:00:44.885 --> 00:00:49.729
Even the members of the band, who had toured all over Europe, felt overwhelmed by the reaction of

12
00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:50.630
the American fans.

13
00:00:51.471 --> 00:00:57.496
It was February 7, 1964, a day that would change the face of popular culture forever.

14
00:00:58.272 --> 00:01:03.196
By the time the Beatles arrived in America, they had already earned quite a reputation for themselves in

15
00:01:03.276 --> 00:01:09.720
Europe. They were well-known musicians by 1962, when they signed a contract with a record label, EMI Records,

16
00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.222
and recorded at London's Abbey Road Studios for the first time.

17
00:01:13.963 --> 00:01:19.807
Despite their popularity in Europe, the band was surprised at the extraordinary welcome they received in the United

18
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:21.668
States and the success that would follow.

19
00:01:22.469 --> 00:01:26.952
Their song "Love Me Do" had peaked at only number 17 on the British pop charts.

20
00:01:27.564 --> 00:01:33.708
But the Beatles quickly put together a second single, "Please Please Me," and this time, the song reached

21
00:01:33.888 --> 00:01:35.229
number two in the United Kingdom.

22
00:01:35.889 --> 00:01:41.872
When their first album, also titled Please Please Me, was released in the UK in 1963, it was

23
00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:45.815
an instant hit, making the members of the band international celebrities.

24
00:01:46.795 --> 00:01:51.778
It was obvious from the very beginning that the Beatles were not just an average pop band; they

25
00:01:51.818 --> 00:01:58.445
were charismatic, talented, and most importantly, willing to experiment with a rock-and-roll sound.

26
00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:05.847
In 1960s Europe, many American rock-and-roll musicians had become quite popular, but the Beatles brought a unique quality

27
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:06.468
to the genre.

28
00:02:06.988 --> 00:02:12.450
Their tunes incorporated aspects of Celtic folk music, giving them a livelier, more rebellious flavor.

29
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:18.712
Even though the lyrics and melodies were quite simple, their songs spoke directly to the dreams and desires

30
00:02:18.752 --> 00:02:19.412
of their fans.

31
00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:26.406
Teenage girls, in particular, were crazy about them, while young boys and audiophiles appreciated their musical skills.

32
00:02:27.066 --> 00:02:30.089
Frenzied fans appeared whenever and wherever they performed.

33
00:02:30.589 --> 00:02:32.531
The media called this "Beatlemania."

34
00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:38.896
The Beatles attracted the attention of Ed Sullivan, a famous American personality, who invited them to perform on

35
00:02:38.937 --> 00:02:41.058
his variety show on national television.

36
00:02:41.679 --> 00:02:47.364
On February 10, their performance was broadcast into the living rooms of millions of viewers across the continent.

37
00:02:47.936 --> 00:02:52.038
This was the first time that most North Americans had ever seen the British group and their distinctive

38
00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:58.021
sense of fashion: they wore black suits with ties and had collar-length, straight hair falling over their eyes

39
00:02:58.081 --> 00:03:02.483
and growing over their ears, a radical departure from the typical hairstyles of the day.

40
00:03:03.143 --> 00:03:09.106
Within days, young men were requesting these "mop-top" haircuts from their barbers and wearing clothes that were similar

41
00:03:09.146 --> 00:03:10.526
in style to those of the band.

42
00:03:11.086 --> 00:03:16.954
Meanwhile, "Please Please Me" was topping the charts, and their record company released a new version of their

43
00:03:17.014 --> 00:03:18.295
album for the American market.

44
00:03:19.216 --> 00:03:24.981
On the cover, the Beatles wore black turtlenecks; days later, these also became hot items, as did the

45
00:03:25.021 --> 00:03:27.683
style of leather cap worn by John during their tour.

46
00:03:28.244 --> 00:03:32.107
Companies even began selling Beatle wigs and costumes to eager customers.

47
00:03:32.908 --> 00:03:37.592
Anything and everything British was considered cool, hip, and desirable.

48
00:03:38.196 --> 00:03:43.459
Not only did the Beatles revolutionize fashion in the United States, but they also changed the way that

49
00:03:43.519 --> 00:03:45.280
the general public appreciated music.

50
00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:51.724
Fondness for the Beatles encouraged enthusiasts to explore other British musicians, such as the Rolling Stones and the

51
00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:58.247
Who. Many journalists called the success of so many UK musicians the "British Invasion" and commented on the

52
00:03:58.287 --> 00:04:02.250
reversal of an earlier trend, when American groups had achieved stardom in the UK.

53
00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:08.994
For two years, from 1964 to 1966, British musicians dominated global music charts.

54
00:04:09.954 --> 00:04:15.358
The Beatles, however, decided that it was time to grow up and produce more mature and diverse albums.

55
00:04:16.018 --> 00:04:19.921
They stopped touring in 1966 in order to focus on studio work.

56
00:04:20.521 --> 00:04:24.804
George, who had learned to play the sitar, used the instrument on several songs.

57
00:04:25.525 --> 00:04:31.749
Before long, everyone was interested in Indian music, and many young people were learning to play the sitar.

58
00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.992
Some rock historians even credit the Beatles with starting the world music trend.

59
00:04:37.012 --> 00:04:39.174
In 1967, the Beatles released Sgt.

60
00:04:39.214 --> 00:04:44.619
Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band, an album now widely accepted as one of the greatest albums ever recorded.

61
00:04:45.339 --> 00:04:50.364
The album sold like hotcakes, but some critics were hesitant to embrace it, believing that the band had

62
00:04:50.404 --> 00:04:53.426
become too experimental and strayed too far from its roots.

63
00:04:54.107 --> 00:04:58.851
Others heralded the album as a welcome transformation that signaled a step forward for the band.

64
00:04:59.392 --> 00:05:03.075
The impact of the new style on the industry, however, was indisputable.

65
00:05:03.675 --> 00:05:10.211
Unfortunately, conflict was erupting within the band: John and Paul had different opinions about the direction the Beatles

66
00:05:10.231 --> 00:05:10.652
should take.

67
00:05:11.232 --> 00:05:16.255
Paul wanted his wife's father, the tycoon Lee Eastman, to take over the business affairs of the band;

68
00:05:16.876 --> 00:05:22.039
John was preoccupied with the creative side and was skeptical that Eastman could manage a musical group.

69
00:05:22.699 --> 00:05:29.884
The disagreements intensified, and in 1970, Paul officially quit the band—the Beatles were splitting up.

70
00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:34.984
This, however, was only the beginning of new chapters for the "Fab Four," as they were known.

71
00:05:35.664 --> 00:05:39.747
George went on to record numerous solo albums and work on projects with other musicians.

72
00:05:40.268 --> 00:05:42.369
Ringo performed with a number of successful groups.

73
00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:48.634
Paul played with a new band, Wings, until 1981, and released many solo albums in a variety of

74
00:05:48.695 --> 00:05:49.455
styles as well.

75
00:05:50.056 --> 00:05:56.241
John and his wife, Yoko Ono, recorded several new albums together and became involved with the antiwar movement

76
00:05:56.261 --> 00:05:58.242
building in response to the Vietnam War.

77
00:05:59.003 --> 00:06:05.861
One of John's songs, "Imagine," whose lyrics celebrate the possibility of a world at peace, is regarded by

78
00:06:05.921 --> 00:06:09.164
many as the most influential pop song of the twentieth century.

79
00:06:10.084 --> 00:06:15.909
Sadly, John was shot and killed outside his New York apartment on December 8, 1980, by a crazed

80
00:06:15.949 --> 00:06:21.834
admirer. Instead of a funeral, Yoko asked that all of his fans take ten minutes of silence to

81
00:06:21.894 --> 00:06:25.458
pause and remember their hero, his music, and the music of the Beatles.

82
00:06:26.078 --> 00:06:31.808
The following Sunday, in a planned vigil, millions of people around the world, including thirty thousand in his

83
00:06:31.828 --> 00:06:37.254
hometown and one hundred thousand in New York, bowed their heads in silence to reflect on John Lennon

84
00:06:37.394 --> 00:06:37.895
and the Beatles.

85
00:06:38.576 --> 00:06:43.842
With their sublime songs, unique style, and rare creativity, they had changed the world.
